-- S23 Maharashtra prose — kanheri-caves
-- Verdict matrix: 7 go / 1 skip / 4 wait
-- Anchors: 109 Buddhist caves (1st BCE - 11th CE) inside Sanjay Gandhi NP, Borivali Mumbai;
--          Cave 3 Chaitya hall with 8.5m Buddha sculptures and monolithic stone benches for ~50;
--          Cave 11 inscription evidence of 174 CE Satavahana Yajna Sri Satakarni grant;
--          Mahayana + Hinayana mix carved over 12 centuries; Cave 90 water cistern complex;
--          SGNP entry Rs 85 + ASI permit Rs 25; closed Mondays;
--          Mumbai-Borivali station 8km; trail 1.5km from car park to Cave 3;
--          Buddha Purnima Tue May 26 2026 pilgrim peak.

INSERT INTO destination_months (
  destination_id, month, verdict, score, prose_lead, why_go, why_not
) VALUES
('kanheri-caves', 1, 'go', 4, 'Kanheri sits inside Sanjay Gandhi National Park in Borivali, 8km from Borivali railway station and reached via SGNP''s main gate (entry Rs 85) followed by a 6km road or 1.5km uphill trail to the cave cluster. January is the cleanest month — daytime 22-30C, low humidity (60%), and the Mumbai pollution haze thins after early-January cool spells. The 109 caves were carved over 12 centuries (1st BCE through 11th CE) into the basalt headland, with Cave 3 — the Chaitya hall — anchoring most visits with its 8.5m carved Buddha figures flanking the entrance and monolithic stone benches that seated around 50 monks for assembly. Inscriptions in cave 11 record a 174 CE grant by Satavahana ruler Yajna Sri Satakarni. SGNP is closed Mondays; weekday mornings 8am-11am are calm enough to read the cave 11 inscription panels carefully.', 'Mild dry winter, low haze, and SGNP trails in clean dry condition; weekday mornings let you study the 174 CE Satavahana inscription in Cave 11 without crowd interference.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 2, 'go', 4, 'February holds Mumbai''s last clean dry-winter window before March humidity begins to creep in. Daytime stays 24-32C with 55-65% humidity. SGNP trails are at their dry best. Kanheri sees moderate weekend traffic — Borivali residents day-tripping into the park — but weekdays remain quiet enough to walk the full 1.5km trail from car park to cave 3 and then continue another 600m up to caves 11, 36, 41, and 90 (the ancient water-cistern complex that fed the monastic community). The Maghi Buddha-related observance attracts a small monastic-flow but Buddhist anchors in February are minor compared to May''s Buddha Purnima. Mumbai February is also the city''s best weather full stop, so all SGNP traffic patterns hold — weekend crowds, weekday calm.', 'Last clean Mumbai dry-winter month before March humidity; SGNP weekday mornings allow full 1.5km + 600m trail walk through caves 3, 11, 36, 41, and the 90 water-cistern complex without crowd noise.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 3, 'go', 4, 'March is when Mumbai humidity starts loading in but daytime stays bearable — 27-34C with 65-75% humidity. Mornings 6:30am-10am still feel cool inside the basalt cave 3 chaitya hall (interior holds 24-26C even on hot days due to thermal mass). SGNP trails are dry and clear; the 1.5km uphill walk gets sweaty but stays doable. No major March Buddhist anchor. Trail traffic drops noticeably from peak Dec-Feb levels — weekday mornings can be near-empty. The cave 41 image of the Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva (one of Kanheri''s most photographed late-Mahayana panels) catches morning east-facing light from about 7:30am. Pack a torch — cave interiors are dim and the 8.5m cave 3 Buddha sculptures show their carving detail best under raked torch light.', 'Mumbai humidity loading but mornings 6:30am-10am still bearable; weekday trail traffic drops sharply from peak winter, allowing close study of late-Mahayana Avalokiteshvara panels in cave 41.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 4, 'wait', 3, 'April flips Mumbai to humid pre-monsoon discomfort. Daytime 32-36C with 75-80% humidity makes the 1.5km uphill trail genuinely sweaty by 9am. The cave interiors stay around 25-27C — basalt thermal mass works — but reaching them requires committing to the climb in heat. SGNP trails remain dry and clear. Visitor numbers fall to monthly lows between school terms. Early mornings 5:30am-8am (SGNP gate opens 7:30am) remain the only comfortable window. The Buddhist-monastic associations of Kanheri don''t have a specific April anchor, but the cave 3 chaitya hall remains the most rewarding single-cave Mahayana site in greater Mumbai. If you''re a serious heritage visitor accepting the heat, April mid-week mornings give you the caves nearly alone; if not, wait for October.', 'Pre-7:30am gate-opening to 10am window stays workable with empty trails and cool basalt cave interiors at 25-27C; serious heritage visitors get near-private access to cave 3 and 11.', 'After 10am the 32-36C daytime with 75-80% Mumbai humidity makes the 1.5km uphill climb to caves 3 and 11 fatiguing; SGNP trail water sources are minimal.'),
('kanheri-caves', 5, 'wait', 3, 'May is Mumbai''s peak heat-humidity month — 33-36C with 75-85% humidity. Trail climbs are punishing after 9am. Buddha Purnima falls Tue May 26 2026 — the most important Theravada and Mahayana lunar anniversary (Vesak), commemorating Buddha''s birth, enlightenment, and parinirvana on the same full-moon date. Kanheri''s cave 3 sees a pilgrim flow of Theravada monastics and Maharashtrian Navayana Buddhist followers on Buddha Purnima — robed monks chant in the chaitya hall, offerings are placed at the 8.5m Buddha sculptures, and the 174 CE Satavahana inscription becomes context for full-day darshan. Despite the heat, the cave interior 25-27C and the 8.5m Buddha sculptures lit by ceremonial lamps make May 26 specifically worth the discomfort. Outside May 26, the month is best avoided.', 'Buddha Purnima Tue May 26 2026 brings full Theravada and Navayana Buddhist pilgrim flow to cave 3 with monastic chanting, ceremonial lamps, and contextual reading of the 174 CE Satavahana inscription panel.', 'Outside the May 26 Buddha Purnima window, 33-36C with 75-85% humidity makes the 1.5km uphill trail genuinely difficult after 9am; SGNP closes mid-month for periodic heat advisories.'),
('kanheri-caves', 6, 'wait', 3, 'June brings the southwest monsoon to Mumbai — typical arrival Jun 10-15. First 7-10 days are usually false-start showers; once consolidated, SGNP receives 600-800mm in June alone. The park stays open but trails turn muddy fast, leech activity ramps up after Jun 15, and slip risk on the 1.5km uphill climb to cave 3 increases sharply. SGNP issues periodic closure alerts during heavy spells (typically 2-3 days/month). The basalt headland with monsoon-fed cascades feeding the ancient cave-90 cistern complex is visually striking — Kanheri shows you why Buddhist monastics chose this hillside in the first place. Early June (Jun 1-10) before monsoon consolidation is workable; the rest of the month is a calculated gamble depending on rain pattern.', 'Early June (1-10) pre-monsoon window stays workable; the basalt headland with cascading water around the ancient cave-90 cistern complex shows the monastic site-selection logic dramatically.', 'After Jun 15 monsoon consolidation triggers muddy trails, leech activity, slip risk on the cave 3 climb, and periodic 2-3 day SGNP closures during heavy spells.'),
('kanheri-caves', 7, 'skip', 2, 'July is Mumbai''s deepest monsoon month — SGNP receives 900-1100mm in July alone. The park stays open between rain alerts but the trail to Kanheri becomes a genuine wet-weather hike with mud sections, leeches in profusion (especially Jul 10-31), slip-prone basalt, and frequent 1-2 day closures during heavy spells. Cave interiors stay dry (basalt overhangs work) but reaching them is the problem. The waterfall around cave 3 chaitya is photographic, and the cave-90 cistern overflows dramatically, but the access risk-to-reward ratio is poor. Mumbai school holidays mean a brief mid-month traffic spike of casual visitors on dry days. Wait for September monsoon-tail or October post-monsoon for clean Kanheri access.', NULL, 'July 900-1100mm Mumbai rainfall turns the SGNP trail to Kanheri into a leech-heavy mud slog with regular 1-2 day park closures; cave interiors stay dry but reaching them is the genuine problem.'),
('kanheri-caves', 8, 'wait', 3, 'August is Mumbai''s second-deepest monsoon month at 750-900mm. The pattern of muddy trails, leeches, and periodic SGNP closures continues. However, by late August the waterfalls around cave 3 are at peak flow — the basalt headland''s monsoon-fed cascades fall directly past the chaitya entrance, and the cave-90 cistern complex shows the engineering logic of ancient Buddhist water harvesting. For determined heritage visitors willing to accept wet-weather hiking and accept that 1-2 days/month see closures, August offers a uniquely atmospheric Kanheri — green forest cover full, cave faces wet and dark, almost no other visitors. Pack good waterproof boots, leech socks, and a torch. Borivali approach is fine; the issue is the in-park 1.5km uphill walk.', 'Late-August waterfalls around cave 3 chaitya are at peak flow and the cave-90 cistern shows the ancient water-harvesting engineering at full operation; near-zero other visitors makes for unusually atmospheric Buddhist site-study.', 'August 750-900mm rainfall continues mud, leech, and slip risk on the 1.5km uphill trail; SGNP issues periodic closures; the visit requires waterproof commitment most casual visitors won''t want.'),
('kanheri-caves', 9, 'go', 4, 'September is the monsoon-tail clarity window. Rain tapers from Sep 15 onward, leech activity drops, SGNP trails dry out by Sep 25-30, and forest cover stays at full monsoon-fed green. Daytime 26-30C with 75-80% humidity in early month, falling to 70-75% by month-end. The waterfalls around cave 3 are still flowing in early September; by month-end they''re reduced but the basalt is still wet-dark. Visitor numbers stay low — Mumbai weekend day-trippers haven''t returned in force yet. This is a transition month that rewards flexibility: target Sep 18-30 if you want dry trails, accept Sep 1-15 if you want active waterfalls. Cave 11''s 174 CE Satavahana inscription is best read in clear post-monsoon light — late September works well.', 'Monsoon-tail clarity emerges with drying trails, dropping leech activity, full green canopy, and still-flowing cave-3 waterfalls in early month; visitor numbers stay low before October weekend tourism ramps.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 10, 'go', 5, 'October is Kanheri''s post-monsoon peak. Daytime 28-32C with 65-75% humidity but rapidly dropping after Oct 15. SGNP trails dry out fully; forest cover stays full green from the recent monsoon refill. Mumbai air clarity peaks — the basalt headland views toward the surrounding city stretch unobstructed for the first time since pre-monsoon. The cave 41 Avalokiteshvara panel and cave 3 Buddha sculptures photograph cleanly in October''s low-angle morning light. Weekend visitor numbers rise from Oct 15 as Mumbai families return to SGNP. Weekday mornings (Tue-Fri, 7:30am-10am) remain the prime study window. Sharad Purnima (Kojagiri) Oct 25 2026 lifts SGNP weekend traffic briefly. The dramatic basalt headland silhouettes against clear October skies make this the best photography month for Kanheri.', 'Post-monsoon clarity peaks with dry SGNP trails, full green canopy, glass-clear Mumbai air, and ideal low-angle morning light on cave-3 Buddha sculptures and the cave-41 Avalokiteshvara panel.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 11, 'go', 5, 'November is Kanheri''s most comfortable month. Daytime 26-30C, nights 18-22C, low humidity (55-65%), zero rain, and Mumbai air clarity at its annual best. SGNP trails are firm and dry; the 1.5km uphill walk to cave 3 feels easy in cool dry air. Tulsi Vivah (Nov 1 2026) and Kartik Purnima (Nov 24) anchor the wider devotional calendar but neither pulls Buddhist-specific traffic to Kanheri. Weekend visitor counts are at annual peak — book Sat-Sun visits with early-morning gate-arrival strategy. Weekday mornings 7:30am-11am remain the calm window. Cave 11 inscription, cave 3 chaitya, cave 41 Avalokiteshvara, cave 90 cistern complex — the full study circuit is comfortable in a single 4-hour visit. Migratory birds (oriental honey buzzard, brahminy starling) appear in SGNP from mid-month.', 'Coolest, driest, clearest month with firm trails and ideal study conditions for the full cave 3 to cave 90 circuit in a single 4-hour visit; SGNP at peak post-monsoon green health.', NULL),
('kanheri-caves', 12, 'go', 5, 'December locks in Mumbai''s winter best — daytime 25-29C, nights 16-20C, near-zero humidity, no rain. SGNP traffic peaks year-round as the city''s only major green-space destination. Weekend gate queues at SGNP main entrance start by 8am — arrive 7am for Sat-Sun visits. Weekday mornings 7:30am-10am remain comfortable. The cave 3 chaitya hall interior at 22-24C is cool enough to sit and study for an hour. December morning light gives the 8.5m Buddha sculptures their best photographic conditions — sun angle 7:30am-9am rakes across carved relief detail. Margashirsha Purnima (Dec 24 2026) is a minor Hindu anchor that lifts SGNP general traffic but not Buddhist-specific footfall. Christmas-New Year week sees Mumbai family day-tripping at annual peak; mid-month (Dec 5-15) stays closer to November-calm.', 'Cool dry Mumbai winter at its annual best, with December morning sun angle giving optimal raking light on the 8.5m cave-3 Buddha sculptures and the 174 CE Satavahana inscription panels.', NULL)
